<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout xml:id="day-11-notes">
  <title>Day 11: Sections 4.3 and 4.4a: Absolute/Local Extrema of Functions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Last Time:</term> We finished learning about our derivative rules and started practicing combining them. We also studied absolute/local extrema geometrically.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Today:</term> We explore how derivatives can be used to determine the absolute/local maxima/minima of a function algebraically.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="def-critical-points">
      <title>Definition: Critical Points</title>
      <p>
        Often the first step is to check critical points.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Definition:</term> Critical points are <fillin characters="14"/> points <m>c</m> in the <fillin characters="14"/> of <m>f(x)</m> where <fillin characters="14"/> or where <fillin characters="18"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Notes:</term> The only places, in the domain of <m>f(x)</m>, where the function can possibly have a(n) <fillin characters="14"/> value (local or absolute), if they exist, are at:
      </p>
      <ol marker="i)">
        <li>
          <p><fillin characters="14"/> points</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>OR</strong> at the <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-cubic-extrema">
      <title>Problem 1</title>
      <p>
        Let's look at the cubic function <m>f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 1</m>.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li>
          <p>Find all critical points of <m>f</m>.</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find <fillin characters="6"/>.</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Solve <fillin characters="10"/>.</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine where <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>Step 4:</strong> Understand the <fillin characters="10"/>, which may be given.</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>Step 5:</strong> Conclude the <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.8in">
          <p>
            Make a sign chart for <m>f'</m> and use this to decide whether each of the critical points you found is a local minimum, a local maximum, or neither. Recall that the values of <m>f'</m> are closely related to whether the function is increasing or decreasing.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="thm-first-derivative-test">
      <title>First Derivative Test</title>
      <p>
        The process we just did in Problem 1(b) is called the <fillin characters="10"/> Derivative Test to decide whether a critical point is a <em>local</em> minimum, a <em>local</em> maximum, or <em>neither</em>. Let's study it here:
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>First Derivative Test:</term> Suppose that <m>c</m> is a <fillin characters="22"/> of a continuous function <m>f(x)</m>, and that <m>f(x)</m> is differentiable at every point in some interval containing <m>c</m> except possibly at <m>c</m> itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Moving across this interval from left to right:
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.8in">
        <strong>Case 1:</strong> If <m>f'(x)</m> changes sign from negative to positive at <m>x = c</m>, <m>f(x)</m> has a <fillin characters="6"/> <fillin characters="12"/> at <m>x = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.8in">
        <strong>Case 2:</strong> If <m>f'(x)</m> changes sign from positive to negative at <m>x = c</m>, <m>f(x)</m> has a <fillin characters="6"/> <fillin characters="12"/> at <m>x = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.8in">
        <strong>Case 3:</strong> If <m>f'(x)</m> goes from positive to positive or negative to negative, <m>f(x)</m> has <fillin characters="5"/> <fillin characters="6"/> <fillin characters="12"/> at <m>x = c</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-cubic-extrema-revisit">
      <title>Problem 1 - Revisit</title>
      <p>
        Next we will see what the second derivative can tell you about local extrema. Let's look at the cubic function <m>f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 1</m>.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li>
          <p>
            Find all critical points of <m>f</m>. <em>Answer: Applying the 5-step strategy, <m>f(x)</m> has critical points at <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 0</m>.</em>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Make a sign chart for <m>f'</m> and use this to decide whether each of the critical points you found is a local minimum, a local maximum, or neither. <em>Answer: Using testing points on <m>f'</m> and increasing/decreasing properties of <m>f</m>,</em>
          </p>
          <sidebyside width="55%">
            <image source="pictures/maxminpic2.png">
              <shortdescription>
                Sign chart for the first derivative f'(x) of f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 1 showing critical points at x = -2 and x = 0 with positive, negative, and positive intervals.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </sidebyside>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.2in">
          <p>
            Now, determine the signs of <m>f''(-2)</m> and <m>f''(0)</m>. What do these signs indicate about the concavity of the function at those points?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-x5-x4-concavity">
      <title>Problem 2</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x) = x^5</m> and <m>g(x) = x^4</m>. The graphs of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> are shown below.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="3% 3%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/sec4143p4.png">
          <shortdescription>Graph of f(x) = x^5 passing through the origin with a horizontal inflection point at x = 0.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <image source="pictures/sec4143p3.png">
          <shortdescription>Graph of g(x) = x^4 showing a U-shaped curve with a global and local minimum at the origin (0, 0).</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>

      <ol marker="a)">
        <li>
          <p>From the graphs, we observe the following:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has <fillin characters="6"/> <fillin characters="10"/> <fillin characters="18"/> at <m>x = 0</m>, while</p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(x)</m> has <fillin characters="6"/> <fillin characters="10"/> <fillin characters="18"/> at <m>x = 0</m>.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Additionally,</p>
          <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" valign="top">
            <p workspace="1.2in"><strong>b-i)</strong> What is <m>f'(0)</m>? <m>f''(0)</m>?</p>
            <p workspace="1.2in"><strong>b-ii)</strong> What is <m>g'(0)</m>? <m>g''(0)</m>?</p>
          </sidebyside>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="thm-second-derivative-test">
      <title>Second Derivative Test</title>
      <p>
        Let's summarize what we have learned about the <fillin characters="8"/> Derivative Test from Problems 1(c) and 2.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Second Derivative Test:</term> Suppose <m>f''</m> is continuous on an open interval that contains <m>x = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.8in">
        <strong>Case 1:</strong> If <m>f'(c) = 0</m> <strong>and</strong> <m>f''(c) \gt 0</m>, <m>f(x)</m> has a <fillin characters="8"/> <fillin characters="18"/> at <m>x = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.8in">
        <strong>Case 2:</strong> If <m>f'(c) = 0</m> <strong>and</strong> <m>f''(c) \lt 0</m>, <m>f(x)</m> has a <fillin characters="8"/> <fillin characters="18"/> at <m>x = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.6in">
        <strong>Case 3:</strong> If <m>f'(c) = 0</m> <strong>and</strong> <m>f''(c) = 0</m>, the test is <fillin characters="18"/>. That is, at <m>x = c</m>, <m>f(x)</m> may have <fillin characters="22"/>, <fillin characters="22"/>, or <fillin characters="10"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-second-deriv-test-ex">
      <title>Problem 3</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x) = x - e^x</m>. Find all critical points of <m>f(x)</m>, and determine whether each critical point is a local minimum, local maximum, or neither by using the Second Derivative Test.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recall the 5-step strategy for finding critical points:
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find <fillin characters="6"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Solve <fillin characters="10"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine where <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 4:</strong> Understand the <fillin characters="10"/>, which may be given.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 5:</strong> Conclude the <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="1.3in">
        Next, we apply the Second Derivative Test to determine local extrema, as requested.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-first-vs-second-deriv-test">
      <title>Problem 4</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>g(x) = 3\,x^{1/3} + 4x</m>. Find all critical points of <m>g(x)</m>, and determine whether each critical point is a local minimum, local maximum, or neither.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recall the 5-step strategy for finding critical points:
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find <fillin characters="6"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Solve <fillin characters="10"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine where <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 4:</strong> Understand the <fillin characters="10"/>, which may be given.</p>
      <p workspace="0.35in"><strong>Step 5:</strong> Conclude the <fillin characters="18"/>.</p>
      <p workspace="1.3in">
        Now, which test should be used to determine local extrema in this case?
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
</handout>